{"step_index":645,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:42Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:06:42Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:06:42Z\nShowing lines 1-309 of 309\n1: ---\n2: name: ovs-db-analysis\n3: description: >-\n4:   Analyze Open vSwitch data from sosreport — conf.db, OpenFlow dumps, port stats,\n5:   tunnels, and datapath health. Use when troubleshooting OVS packet drops, flow\n6:   performance, bridge/port/interface issues, DPDK vs kernel datapath, or when the\n7:   user mentions sosreport openvswitch, ovs-ofctl, ovsdb-tool, or ovs-db-analysis.\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # OVS Database and Flow Analysis\n11: \n12: This skill provides detailed analysis of Open vSwitch (OVS) data collected via sosreport. It operates in four modes:\n13: \n14: 1. **Default mode**: Full analysis - conf.db + all text files (requires ovsdb-tool, falls back to text files if not available)\n15: 2. **Database mode (`--db`)**: Database only - analyze conf.db (requires ovsdb-tool)\n16: 3. **Text files mode (`--flows-only`)**: Text files only - no ovsdb-tool needed\n17: 4. **Query mode (`--query`)**: Run custom OVSDB JSON queries (requires ovsdb-tool)\n18: \n19: ## When to Use This Skill\n20: \n21: Use this skill when:\n22: - **Troubleshooting packet drops** - Find drop flows being hit\n23: - **Analyzing flow performance** - View top flows by packet count\n24: - Analyzing OVS configuration from a sosreport\n25: - Troubleshooting bridge, port, or interface issues\n26: - Investigating DPDK vs kernelspace datapath\n27: - Reviewing tunnel configurations (VXLAN, Geneve, GRE)\n28: - Checking port statistics (drops, errors, traffic)\n29: - Mapping Kubernetes pods to OVS interfaces\n30: \n31: ## Prerequisites\n32: \n33: **Default mode** (full analysis):\n34: - **ovsdb-tool** must be installed (from openvswitch package)\n35: - Falls back to `--flows-only` if ovsdb-tool not found\n36: - Sosreport with `sos_commands/openvswitch/` directory and `conf.db`\n37: \n38: **Database mode** (`--db`):\n39: - **ovsdb-tool** must be installed (from openvswitch package)\n40:   - Check: `which ovsdb-tool`\n41:   - Fedora/RHEL: `sudo dnf install openvswitch`\n42:   - Ubuntu/\n<truncated 9170 bytes>\n=============\n256: \n257:   System UUID: 7e9a3f70-86fa-4578-a849-4fd807a64a10\n258:   Total Bridges: 2\n259: \n260:   Bridge: br-ex\n261:     ports: 3\n262:       internal: br-ex\n263:       patch: patch-br-ex-to-br-int\n264:       system: nm-bond\n265: \n266:   Bridge: br-int\n267:     fail_mode: secure\n268:     datapath_type: system\n269:     ports: 12\n270:       geneve: 9 ports\n271:       internal: ovn-k8s-mp0, br-int\n272:       patch: patch-br-int-to-br-ex\n273: \n274: ================================================================================\n275: OPENFLOW ANALYSIS\n276: ================================================================================\n277: \n278:   Bridge: br-int\n279:   ----------------------------------------------------------------------\n280:   Total flows: 2,017\n281:   Flows with hits: 318\n282:   Drop flows: 150 (9 actively dropping)\n283: \n284:   ⚠️  ACTIVE DROP FLOWS (9):\n285:     table=40, priority=0, packets=8,105\n286:     table=79, priority=100, packets=1,356\n287:       match: ip,reg14=0x2,metadata=0x5,dl_src=00:62:0b:ea:b5:e0\n288: \n289: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n290: DATAPATH FLOW TABLE HEALTH\n291: --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n292: \n293:   Current flows: 155 / 200,000 (0.1% used)\n294:   Average: 156, Max seen: 215\n295:   ✓ Flow table healthy\n296: ```\n297: \n298: ## Error Handling\n299: \n300: | Error | Solution |\n301: |-------|----------|\n302: | `ovsdb-tool not found` | Install openvswitch package OR use default mode (no --db) |\n303: | `conf.db not found` | Use default mode (analyzes text files instead) |\n304: | `sos_commands/openvswitch not found` | Ensure sosreport has OVS data collected |\n305: \n306: ## See Also\n307: \n308: - [OVN Database Analysis](../../must-gather/skills/must-gather-analyzer/scripts/analyze_ovn_dbs.py) - For OVN NB/SB databases\n309: - [sosreport openvswitch plugin](https://github.com/sosreport/sos/blob/main/sos/report/plugins/openvswitch.py) - What sosreport collects","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":646,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:42Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:06:42Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:06:42Z\nShowing lines 1-126 of 126\n1: ---\n2: name: btrfs\n3: description: >\n4:   Btrfs filesystem administration: subvolumes, snapshots, rollback, balance,\n5:   scrub, RAID profiles, compression, quotas, send/receive backups, and\n6:   troubleshooting.\n7:   MUST consult when installing, configuring, or troubleshooting btrfs.\n8: triggerPhrases:\n9:   - \"btrfs\"\n10:   - \"Btrfs\"\n11:   - \"btrfs snapshot\"\n12:   - \"btrfs subvolume\"\n13:   - \"btrfs balance\"\n14:   - \"btrfs scrub\"\n15:   - \"copy-on-write filesystem\"\n16:   - \"CoW filesystem\"\n17:   - \"btrfs-progs\"\n18:   - \"mkfs.btrfs\"\n19:   - \"snapper\"\n20:   - \"btrbk\"\n21: globs:\n22:   - \"**/fstab\"\n23:   - \"**/etc/fstab\"\n24: last_verified: \"unverified\"\n25: ---\n26: \n27: ## Identity\n28: \n29: | Property | Value |\n30: |----------|-------|\n31: | **Type** | Kernel built-in filesystem (no loadable module needed on most distros) |\n32: | **CLI tool** | `btrfs` (subcommands: `filesystem`, `subvolume`, `balance`, `scrub`, `device`, `quota`, `qgroup`, `check`, `defragment`, `rescue`) |\n33: | **Distro install** | `apt install btrfs-progs` / `dnf install btrfs-progs` |\n34: | **Create** | `mkfs.btrfs` |\n35: | **Kernel version matters** | Significant stability improvements landed between 4.x and 6.x; older kernels have known bugs with RAID-5/6 and balance |\n36: \n37: ## Quick Start\n38: \n39: ```bash\n40: sudo apt install btrfs-progs\n41: sudo mkfs.btrfs -L mydata /dev/sdX1\n42: sudo mount /dev/sdX1 /mnt/mydata\n43: btrfs filesystem show /mnt/mydata\n44: btrfs device stats /mnt/mydata\n45: ```\n46: \n47: ## Key Operations\n48: \n49: | Task | Command |\n50: |------|---------|\n51: | Show filesystems | `btrfs filesystem show` |\n52: | Filesystem usage (detailed) | `btrfs filesystem usage /mount` |\n53: | List subvolumes | `btrfs subvolume list /mount` |\n54: | Create subvolume | `btrfs subvolume create /mount/name` |\n55: | Delete subvolume | `btrfs subvolume delete /mount/name` |\n56: | Create read-write snapshot | `btrfs subvolume snapshot /mount/src /mount\n<truncated 4897 bytes>\n decreases after snapshot deletion completes.\n109: - **`btrfs check` is slow and `--repair` is risky**: Unlike `fsck.ext4`, Btrfs check does not have a safe repair mode. `--repair` can corrupt a filesystem that `check` reports as damaged. Prefer `btrfs rescue` subcommands (`zero-log`, `fix-device-size`) for targeted fixes.\n110: - **Btrfs RAID-5/6 has known parity issues**: As of kernels through 6.x, Btrfs RAID-5 and RAID-6 have unresolved write-hole bugs. Do not use RAID-5/6 for data you care about. Use RAID-1 or RAID-10 instead.\n111: - **Send/receive for backups**: `btrfs send` / `btrfs receive` is efficient for incremental backups using read-only snapshots as base references. Tools like `snapper` and `btrbk` automate the snapshot lifecycle and send/receive workflow — prefer them over manual scripting.\n112: - **CoW fragmentation on databases and VMs**: Copy-on-write writes new data to new locations, which fragments sequential files over time. Use `nodatacow` mount option (or `chattr +C`) for VM disk images, database files, and other large randomly-written files. `nodatacow` also disables checksums for those files.\n113: \n114: ## See Also\n115: \n116: - **ext4** — Traditional Linux filesystem without CoW overhead; use when you need maximum compatibility and don't need snapshots\n117: - **xfs** — High-performance filesystem for large sequential I/O; use for databases and media workloads where CoW fragmentation is a concern\n118: - **zfs** — Full storage stack with pooling and replication; use when you need enterprise-grade checksums and send/receive without kernel version sensitivity\n119: - **lvm** — Logical volume manager; provides snapshot and resize capabilities at the block layer beneath btrfs\n120: \n121: ## References\n122: \n123: See `references/` for:\n124: - `btrfs-options.md` — mkfs and mount options organized by category\n125: - `common-patterns.md` — filesystem setup, subvolume layouts, snapshots, rollback, backup, RAID, and compression\n126: - `docs.md` — official documentation and community links","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":647,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:42Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:06:42Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:06:42Z\nShowing lines 1-213 of 213\n1: ---\n2: name: incus\n3: description: >\n4:   Incus system containers and VMs: instance/profile YAML, disk devices,\n5:   native storage pools and volumes, Btrfs subvolumes, lifecycle and isolation.\n6:   Consult before creating or changing an Incus instance, profile, disk,\n7:   storage volume, root binding or provisioning helper.\n8: triggerPhrases:\n9:   - \"Incus\"\n10:   - \"Incus disk\"\n11:   - \"Incus profile\"\n12:   - \"Incus storage\"\n13:   - \"container root disk\"\n14:   - \"native storage volume\"\n15: last_verified: \"2026-10-19\"\n16: ---\n17: \n18: # Incus: configuration first, storage-driver ownership\n19: \n20: Project-local skill authored from the official Incus documentation below;\n21: not an imported third-party skill or a container-sandbox wrapper. Documentation\n22: review is not proof that any example has been applied to this host. Check the\n23: installed client/server version and applicable API extensions before using\n24: version-dependent options.\n25: \n26: ## Start with the actual declaration\n27: \n28: An Incus instance is configured through instance properties, `config`,\n29: `devices` and ordered `profiles`. The CLI exposes this as YAML; do not invent\n30: a separate authoritative YAML file on disk. Incus owns its configuration.\n31: \n32: Read both local and expanded instance configuration. Later profiles override\n33: earlier ones; instance-local configuration overrides profiles. Editing a\n34: shared profile affects every instance that uses it. Do not use expanded\n35: configuration as a blind replacement: it flattens inherited settings.\n36: \n37: Read-only infrastructure diagnostics, within the assigned maintenance scope:\n38: \n39: ```sh\n40: incus version\n41: incus config show <instance>\n42: incus config show <instance> --expanded\n43: incus profile show <profile>\n44: incus storage show <pool>\n45: incus storage volume list <pool>\n46: incus storage volume show <pool> container/<instance>\n47: ```\n48: \n49: Confirm the selected remo\n<truncated 8719 bytes>\nt size/inode/operation result. A created image\n186:   does not establish Btrfs membership or migrate Cozo data into that image.\n187: - Distinguish direct regular-file rejection from a provisioning-policy denial.\n188:   Installed btrfs-progs may reject a regular `.img` with `is not a block device`;\n189:   report that real result rather than claiming completion or silently creating\n190:   a loop/device-mapper alternative against the owner's instruction. Full\n191:   allocation does not change a regular file's type: if a nonsparse retry is\n192:   explicitly requested, verify allocated bytes (disable compression on the\n193:   empty file before writing zeros), and do not repeat that allocation after\n194:   the same type rejection.\n195: - Reconcile matching creation receipts and verify the retained storage binding\n196:   on repeats. Normal lifecycle operations must not repeat device-add, format\n197:   storage or recreate roots. Interrupted intent requires reconciliation, not\n198:   an automatic new allocation.\n199: - Reuse existing identity, authorization and policy services. Do not build\n200:   parallel services, mint new credentials, broaden grants or edit Cozo\n201:   directly as part of OS provisioning. Verify the mounted filesystem and\n202:   authoritative binding before claiming persistence is configured.\n203: \n204: ## Official references\n205: \n206: Consult the version matching the installed daemon where behavior differs.\n207: These upstream pages were reviewed for this skill on 2026-10-19:\n208: \n209: - [Instance configuration](https://linuxcontainers.org/incus/docs/main/explanation/instance_config/)\n210: - [Profiles and inheritance](https://linuxcontainers.org/incus/docs/main/profiles/)\n211: - [Disk devices](https://linuxcontainers.org/incus/docs/main/reference/devices_disk/)\n212: - [Btrfs storage driver](https://linuxcontainers.org/incus/docs/main/reference/storage_btrfs/)\n213: - [Storage volume creation, attachment and content-type restrictions](https://linuxcontainers.org/incus/docs/main/howto/storage_volumes/)","truncated_fields":["content"]}
