{"step_index":649,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:47Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:06:47Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:06:48Z\nShowing lines 1-423 of 423\n1: ---\n2: name: kernel-fuse-diagnosis\n3: description: >\n4:   FUSE（用户态文件系统）内核端故障诊断技能。当用户提到 FUSE 文件系统 hang、\n5:   所有操作返回 EIO、FUSE daemon 崩溃/重启、请求队列阻塞、D 状态进程堆积、\n6:   /sys/fs/fuse/connections/ 异常、libfuse 兼容性、max_read/max_write 配置不当、\n7:   writeback cache 一致性问题、多线程 FUSE daemon 死锁、容器中 /dev/fuse 权限问题、\n8:   FUSE 性能退化等关键词时，必须使用本技能。\n9:   覆盖场景：FUSE daemon 崩溃后 EIO、请求队列阻塞与 D 状态、max_read/max_write 配置不当、\n10:   writeback cache 一致性问题、多线程 daemon 死锁、/dev/fuse 权限问题、内核 FUSE 模块 Bug、\n11:   混合复杂 FUSE 故障。即使只收到\"文件系统挂了\"、\"目录打不开\"、\"ls 卡死\"但挂载点\n12:   为 FUSE 类型时也应触发本技能。\n13:   支持 FUSE 全链路诊断（sysfs 队列 → 内核参数 → daemon 进程 → 代码级根因）。\n14: ---\n15: \n16: # FUSE 内核端故障诊断（三层下钻：系统层 → 类型层 → 代码根因层）\n17: \n18: ## 第一节：故障目录结构\n19: \n20: ```text\n21: fuse_case/                  # 故障案例目录\n22: ├── scripts/                # 【内置】本技能的诊断脚本\n23: │   ├── 01_baseline_info.sh\n24: │   ├── branch_A_daemon_crash.sh\n25: │   ├── branch_B_req_queue.sh\n26: │   ├── branch_C_max_read_write.sh\n27: │   ├── branch_D_writeback_cache.sh\n28: │   ├── branch_E_mt_deadlock.sh\n29: │   ├── branch_F_dev_fuse_perm.sh\n30: │   ├── branch_G_kernel_bug.sh\n31: │   └── branch_H_mixed.sh\n32: ├── logs/                   # 【可选】应用日志\n33: │   ├── application.log\n34: │   └── dmesg_output.txt\n35: ├── fuse_state/             # 【可选】故障时刻的 FUSE 状态快\n<truncated 18428 bytes>\n优先级排序（先解决依赖的下层问题）\n387:     ├── 多故障串联（daemon 崩溃 → 残留连接 → 新 daemon 无法挂载）\n388:     ├── 配置错误叠加（max_read 过小 + 线程池太小 + writeback 未启用）\n389:     ├── 环境变化触发（内核升级、cgroup 限制、SELinux 策略变更）\n390:     └── 复杂的 libfuse API 使用错误（回调注册、会话管理、信号处理）\n391: ```\n392: \n393: **典型输出**：\n394: ```\n395: 结论: 多种故障共存：daemon 线程池过小（4 线程）导致写性能差，\n396:       同时 max_read=4K 进一步退化读性能，writeback cache 启用\n397:       但 daemon 未正确处理 ->write 回调。\n398: 根因: 多重配置叠加导致 FUSE 极端性能退化，建议逐一修复：\n399:       1. 增大线程池至 16+\n400:       2. 设置 max_read=128K+\n401:       3. 检查 writeback cache 回调实现\n402: ```\n403: \n404: ---\n405: \n406: ## 第四节：故障模式速查表\n407: \n408: | 故障模式 | 核心命令 | 特征日志/现象 |\n409: |---------|---------|-------------|\n410: | Daemon 崩溃 EIO | `stat <mount_point>` | `Transport endpoint is not connected` |\n411: | 请求队列阻塞 | `cat .../connections/*/waiting` | `waiting > 0` 且持续增长 |\n412: | max_read/write 不当 | `cat .../connections/*/max_read` | `max_read=4096` 性能异常低 |\n413: | writeback 不一致 | `mount \\| grep writeback_cache` | 写入后读取数据不同 |\n414: | 多线程死锁 | `cat /proc/<pid>/task/*/stack` | `pthread_mutex_lock` 全部阻塞 |\n415: | /dev/fuse 权限 | `ls -la /dev/fuse` | `Permission denied` |\n416: | 内核 Bug | `dmesg \\| grep -i fuse` | `kernel BUG at fs/fuse/dev.c` |\n417: | 混合故障 | 汇总所有诊断 | 多种现象并存 |\n418: \n419: ## 第五节：参考文档\n420: \n421: - `references/fuse_commands.md` — FUSE 诊断命令速查\n422: - `references/fuse_params.md` — 内核 FUSE 相关参数说明\n423: - `references/fuse_patterns.md` — FUSE 故障模式与正则匹配","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":650,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:47Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:06:47Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:06:48Z\nShowing lines 1-75 of 75\n1: ---\n2: name: fuse\n3: description: Build, debug, and review Linux FUSE filesystems with the official libfuse userspace library. Use when implementing a FUSE filesystem, choosing between libfuse high-level vs low-level APIs, wiring callbacks, building with fuse3/pkg-config, mounting for local testing, or troubleshooting fusermount3 and permission issues.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # FUSE\n7: \n8: Use the official `libfuse/libfuse` project as the baseline for Linux FUSE work.\n9: \n10: ## Quick Start\n11: \n12: 1. Confirm the task is Linux FUSE userspace work, not kernel module work.\n13: 2. Prefer the official `libfuse` interfaces and examples before suggesting wrappers.\n14: 3. Decide API style:\n15: - Use the high-level API for simpler path-based filesystems.\n16: - Use the low-level API for inode-level control, explicit replies, or advanced behavior.\n17: 4. Build with `pkg-config fuse3 --cflags --libs` unless the project already uses another build system.\n18: 5. For local runs, prefer foreground debug mode first: `-f -d`.\n19: \n20: ## Workflow\n21: \n22: ### 1. Choose the API\n23: \n24: Use the high-level API when the user needs a straightforward filesystem and path callbacks are sufficient.\n25: Read `include/fuse.h` semantics first.\n26: \n27: Use the low-level API when the user needs inode handling, async-style explicit replies, or finer control over request lifetime.\n28: Read `include/fuse_lowlevel.h` semantics first.\n29: \n30: ### 2. Start from official examples\n31: \n32: Check the `example/` directory in `libfuse/libfuse` before drafting handlers from scratch.\n33: For passthrough, mirror, hello-world, or minimal callback wiring, adapt an official example instead of inventing a new skeleton.\n34: \n35: ### 3. Build correctly\n36: \n37: Use these defaults unless the project already defines them:\n38: \n39: ```bash\n40: pkg-config fuse3 --cflags --libs\n41: ```\n42: \n43: Typical manual build pattern:\n44: \n45: ```bash\n46: gcc fs.c -o fs $(pkg-config fuse3 --cflags --libs)\n47: ```\n48: \n49: If the project vendors `libfuse`, follow its existing Meson/Ninja setup instead of replacing the build flow.\n50: \n51: ### 4. Run and debug safely\n52: \n53: Use a writable, non-sticky mountpoint owned by the current user.\n54: Start in foreground debug mode first:\n55: \n56: ```bash\n57: ./fs -f -d <mountpoint>\n58: ```\n59: \n60: If mounting fails, check:\n61: - `fusermount3` availability\n62: - `/etc/fuse.conf`\n63: - `allow_other` vs `default_permissions`\n64: - whether the mountpoint is writable by the mounting user\n65: \n66: ### 5. Troubleshoot by symptom\n67: \n68: If callbacks are not firing, verify the mount succeeded and the process is still in foreground.\n69: If permission behavior is surprising, inspect `default_permissions` and `allow_other`.\n70: If the filesystem hangs, inspect whether a low-level handler failed to send a reply.\n71: If performance is poor, check whether the design needs low-level API control, cache tuning, or fewer round trips.\n72: \n73: ## Read Next\n74: \n75: Read `references/official-libfuse.md` when you need the official repo, docs, build flow, and the canonical places to look for examples and headers."}
{"step_index":651,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T11:06:48Z","content":"Created At: 2026-10-26T11:06:48Z\nCompleted At: 2026-10-26T11:06:48Z\nShowing lines 1-352 of 352\n1: ---\n2: name: rust-memory-optimization\n3: description: |\n4:   Memory optimization and allocation reduction in Rust. Use when:\n5:   - User is designing memory-sensitive or low-latency systems\n6:   - Optimizing data structures to reduce memory footprint\n7:   - Replacing heap allocations with stack-allocated/bounded structures (SmallVec, ArrayVec, ThinVec)\n8:   - Handling small strings with optimizations (CompactString)\n9:   - Reusing collections via clear() in loops\n10:   - Implementing zero-copy decoding and references\n11:   - Structuring arena-allocated memory (Bumpalo)\n12:   - Measuring memory usage, profiling heap, or diagnosing leaks\n13: ---\n14: \n15: \n16: \n17: ## Quick Navigation\n18: \n19: - [references/stack_allocations.md](references/stack_allocations.md)\n20: - [references/arenas.md](references/arenas.md)\n21: \n22: # Rust Memory Optimization Guide\n23: \n24: Advanced strategies for reducing allocations, shrinking layout sizes, and boosting performance.\n25: \n26: ## Core Rules & Patterns\n27: \n28: ### 1. Bounded Heap-Free Collections\n29: For collections that are usually small, avoid standard vectors to bypass heap allocations.\n30: - **`SmallVec`**: Stack-allocated up to a threshold, overflows to the heap.\n31: - **`ArrayVec`**: Fixed-capacity array wrapper; never allocates on the heap.\n32: - **`ThinVec`**: Only takes a single pointer size when empty, reducing size in enum layouts.\n33: \n34: ```rust\n35: use smallvec::{smallvec, SmallVec};\n36: use arrayvec::ArrayVec;\n37: \n38: // SmallVec: Allocates 4 items on the stack. Goes to heap only if count > 4.\n39: let mut local_tags: SmallVec<[String; 4]> = smallvec![];\n40: local_tags.push(\"Rust\".to_string());\n41: \n42: // ArrayVec: Absolute limit of 8 items on the stack. No heap overflow.\n43: let mut coordinates: ArrayVec<f64, 8> = ArrayVec::new();\n44: coordinates.push(42.0);\n45: ```\n46: \n47: ### 2. Small String Optimization (SSO)\n48: Standard `String` consumes 24 bytes and allocate\n<truncated 7908 bytes>\n`\n306: \n307: ```rust\n308: // Bad: allocating inside a hot loop\n309: for item in items {\n310:     let s = format!(\"processing {}\", item); // allocates every iteration\n311: }\n312: \n313: // Good: reuse a buffer\n314: let mut buf = String::with_capacity(128);\n315: for item in items {\n316:     buf.clear();\n317:     write!(buf, \"processing {}\", item).unwrap();\n318: }\n319: ```\n320: \n321: - Replacing every `Vec` with `SmallVec` without measuring.\n322: - Keeping arena references after reset.\n323: - Cloning strings to satisfy ownership instead of shortening borrow scopes.\n324: - Using `Arc` as a default clone workaround.\n325: - Optimizing layout for types created a handful of times.\n326: - Using `Box<dyn Trait>` when generics would eliminate the allocation entirely.\n327: \n328: ## Review Prompt\n329: \n330: When reviewing memory optimization, ask what was measured, which allocation is removed, what lifetime assumptions changed, and whether the new type increases stack size or API complexity.\n331: \n332: ## Optimization Checklist\n333: \n334: - Measure allocations before changing data structures.\n335: - Preallocate collections only when capacity is known or bounded.\n336: - Prefer borrowing and `Cow` before cloning strings or buffers.\n337: - Use arenas only when lifetimes are truly batch-scoped.\n338: - Check stack growth when replacing heap allocations with inline storage.\n339: - Re-benchmark after changes and keep the simpler version if gains are noise.\n340: - Profile with `dhat` or `heaptrack` to find actual allocation hotspots.\n341: - Consider `hashbrown` or `rustc-hash` for HashMap-heavy code.\n342: \n343: ## References\n344: \n345: - [Rust Performance Book: Heap Allocations](https://nnethercote.github.io/perf-book/heap-allocations.html)\n346: - [smallvec](https://docs.rs/smallvec)\n347: - [arrayvec](https://docs.rs/arrayvec)\n348: - [bumpalo](https://docs.rs/bumpalo)\n349: - [compact_str](https://docs.rs/compact_str)\n350: - [bytes](https://docs.rs/bytes)\n351: - [dhat](https://docs.rs/dhat)\n352: - [hashbrown](https://docs.rs/hashbrown)","truncated_fields":["content"]}
